#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:4
msgid "Security Zones"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:6
msgid "``Infrastructure > Network > Integrations > (select Security Server)``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:9
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:11
msgid ""
"Security zones define logical boundaries within a network security "
"integration that segment traffic and enforce security policies at zone "
"boundaries. Zones are a fundamental concept in firewall architectures "
"where traffic is classified based on its zone membership and policies are"
" applied at inter-zone boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:13
msgid ""
"In |morpheus|, security zones are synced from supported security "
"integrations and can be referenced when configuring firewall rules, "
"security groups, and provisioning workflows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:16
msgid "Zone Concepts"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:22
msgid "Concept"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:23
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:24
msgid "Trust Level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:25
msgid ""
"Zones typically have an associated trust level (e.g., trusted, untrusted,"
" DMZ) that influences default policy behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:26
msgid "Inter-zone Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:27
msgid ""
"Traffic moving between zones is subject to security policies; traffic "
"within a zone may be implicitly permitted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:28
msgid "Zone Membership"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:29
msgid ""
"Network interfaces, segments, or subnets are assigned to zones to "
"classify traffic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:30
msgid "Default Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:31
msgid ""
"The action taken on traffic between zones when no explicit rule matches "
"(typically deny)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:34
msgid "Viewing Security Zones"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:36
msgid ""
"Security zones are visible within the context of their security server "
"integration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:38
msgid "Navigate to ``Infrastructure > Network > Integrations``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:39
msgid "Select the desired security server integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:40
msgid ""
"Zones may be displayed in the **Summary** tab or accessible via a "
"dedicated section depending on the integration type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:43
msgid "Zone-Based Firewall Rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:45
msgid ""
"When creating firewall rules on integrations that support zone-based "
"security, zones can be referenced as source or destination qualifiers:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:47
msgid "Navigate to the **Firewall** tab of the security server integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:48
msgid "Create or edit a firewall rule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:49
msgid "Select the source zone and destination zone for the rule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:50
msgid "Define the action (allow/deny) and service criteria"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:51
msgid "Save the rule"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:53
msgid "Note"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:53
msgid ""
"Zone-based rule support varies by integration type. Check the specific "
"integration guide for your security platform for details on zone "
"configuration and management."
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:56
msgid "Integration-Specific Zone Support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:58
msgid "Palo Alto Networks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:59
msgid ""
"Palo Alto firewalls use security zones as a primary organizational "
"construct. Every interface must be assigned to a zone, and all security "
"policies reference source and destination zones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:61
msgid "NSX-T"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:62
msgid ""
"NSX-T uses transport zones to define the scope of logical networks. "
"Security groups and distributed firewall rules in NSX-T operate "
"independently of transport zones but can reference zone-aware constructs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:64
msgid "Cisco ACI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:65
msgid ""
"ACI uses the concept of security domains and contexts (VRFs) as zone "
"equivalents, providing multi-tenant isolation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:68
msgid "Required Role Permissions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../infrastructure/networks/security/security_zones.rst:70
msgid ""
"Access to security zone viewing requires the ``Infrastructure: Network "
"Integrations`` permission with ``read`` or ``full`` access. Managing "
"zone-based firewall rules requires the ``Infrastructure: Network "
"Firewalls`` permission with ``managerules`` or ``full`` access."
msgstr ""

